{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — confirmed root cause (Phase 1 complete)\n\n### Mechanism\n\n`templatefile()` interpolates the **entire** file as an HCL template. Shell comment syntax is\ninvisible to it: a `#` line is template text like any other. `$${` is the escape for a literal\n`${`; an unescaped `${name}` is an interpolation whose `name` must resolve against the template\nvariable map.\n\n`infra/terraform/main.tf:63` declares exactly **one** variable:\n\n    user_data = templatefile(\"${path.module}/user_data.sh\", {\n      data_volume_id = aws_ebs_volume.data.id\n    })\n\nA scan of `user_data.sh` (proper escape-aware scanner, not a naive grep — it consumes `$$`/`%%`\nas escapes) finds **7** unescaped interpolation sequences, split cleanly in two:\n\n**Legitimate (4)** — all reference the declared `data_volume_id`, all must stay unescaped:\n\n- `:25:42`, `:28:20`, `:53:57`, `:56:28`\n\n**Broken (3)** — reference `PARAMS`, a *bash* associative array that is not a template variable:\n\n- `:92:14`  — `# reduces to ${!PARAMS[@]} / ...`\n- `:92:30`  — `# ... / ${PARAMS[$name]}. ...`\n- `:111:46` — `# After rendering, \"$${!PARAMS[@]}\" becomes \"${!PARAMS[@]}\" ...`\n\nAll three are inside `#` comments, and each escapes the *first* mention (`$${...}`) while leaving\nthe \"reduces to\" / \"becomes\" half unescaped. Terraform then tries to parse `!PARAMS[@]` as an HCL\nexpression — hence `Invalid character; This character is not used within the language` at the `!`.\n\n### Discriminating experiment\n\nIsolated the template evaluation from the rest of the config in `/tmp/tfprobe` (a one-output\n`.tf` calling `templatefile()` on a copy of the file, so nothing but the template is under test):\n\n1. **RED** — unmodified copy reproduces the report verbatim:\n\n       Call to function \"templatefile\" failed: ./user_data.sh:92,24-25: Invalid\n       character; This character is not used within the language., and 3 other\n       diagnostic(s).\n\n2. **GREEN** — escaping *only* those 3 sites (leaving all 4 `data_volume_id` sites untouched)\n   renders the whole file successfully; the plan completes and emits the rendered script.\n\nThat splits the account cleanly: the 3 comment sites are **necessary and sufficient**. It also\nrules out the rival hypothesis \"any `${` in the file breaks it\" — the 4 `data_volume_id` sites are\nunescaped and entirely fine, which is exactly why the regression guard must be\n**declared-variable-aware** rather than a blanket ban on `${`.\n\nIndependently confirmed `terraform init` succeeds and the S3 backend is reachable (the failing run\nacquired and released a real state lock), so remote state is not implicated.\n\n### Defect origin\n\n**`fe4e-54a5-3c3a-4901`** — commit `ef1a7e66a65d` \"Gate standalone shell scripts with ShellCheck\".\n\n`git blame -w -C -M` on the implicated lines attributes 91, 92, 93 and 111 to that commit\n(2026-08-26). Line 90 predates it (`30c92d4f43df`, 2026-06-29) and is *correctly* escaped — the\ncarrier check therefore stops at `ef1a7e66a65d` rather than walking further back. Date test passes:\nthe implicated lines are the same age as the good→bad transition, so this is a genuine\n**regression**, not an unmet obligation inherited from elsewhere.\n\nThe commit message itself shows the awareness gap:\n\n> terraform/user_data.sh: three justified line-scoped disables. It is a templatefile() source, so\n> ShellCheck lints it pre-render and resolves neither the Terraform interpolation nor the\n> `$${...}` escaping.\n\nThe change correctly identified the file as a `templatefile()` source and correctly escaped the\nprose it *quoted*, then wrote a second, unescaped copy of the same expansion in the explanatory\nhalf of each sentence. A reviewer on that ticket could plausibly have caught it, which is the test\nfor a correct attribution.\n\n### Legitimacy: `defect` (regression)\n\nAuthoritative source: `terraform plan` ran green before `ef1a7e66a65d` and fails after, on an\notherwise untouched configuration — an existing behaviour broke. Not a feature request, not\nintended behaviour.\n\n## Fix plan\n\n1. **`infra/terraform/user_data.sh`** — reword the three comment sites so the explanation survives\n   and no unescaped `${` remains. Straight `$$`-escaping of the second half is rejected: it renders\n   both halves identically, turning \"X reduces to X\" into nonsense in the file that actually lands\n   on the box. Instead state the *rule* (and the trap that caused this bug) rather than printing\n   the post-render form a second time.\n\n2. **`scripts/check_templatefile_escapes.py`** (new) — the regression guard. ShellCheck cannot\n   catch this class: the file is valid bash and the breakage is in a different consumer. The guard\n   parses `templatefile(...)` call sites out of the `.tf` files, extracts each call's **declared\n   variable names**, escape-aware-scans the referenced template, and fails on any interpolation\n   whose root identifier is not declared. That is the semantically correct rule — it accepts the\n   4 legitimate `data_volume_id` sites and rejects the 3 broken `PARAMS` ones.\n\n3. **`Makefile`** — wire into `lint`, following the existing `scripts/check_*.py` gate pattern.\n\n4. **`tests/unit/test_check_templatefile_escapes.py`** — RED→GREEN→mutation coverage plus the\n   `make lint` wiring assertion the sibling gates use.\n\nBlast radius is local: the comment edit changes only `user_data`, and\n`user_data_replace_on_change` defaults to `false`, so this is an **in-place update** — it does not\nrebuild the Gerrit instance.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787869828054668681,"uuid":"fb7ed8d1-dd42-46d1-b41f-08c9320dffd8"}